          "policyEvidence": "high",
          "pathExploitability": "low",
          "why": "Grants iam:PassRole together with ec2 action(s) (ec2:RunInstances) that run code as the passed role. A principal can create/update a ec2 workload with a role it is allowed to pass, potentially obtaining execution under the passed role's credentials (whether execution is actually obtained still depends on instance-profile / service behavior). The PassRole grant does not use iam:PassedToService to restrict which supported AWS services may receive the role (AWS still enforces which services a role can be passed to). PassRole target viability is UNKNOWN: the analyzed principal's partition was not supplied, so it cannot be compared to the passed role's partition (an account id does not encode a partition). Whether this is a same-partition (viable) or cross-partition (not viable) pass cannot be established. Reported as unknown viability (fail closed), not a confident not-viable demotion; supply the analyzed principal's partition (aws, aws-us-gov, aws-cn, aws-iso...) to resolve it.",
          "limit": "Capability from this policy alone, not effective access. A single policy cannot establish effective permissions: other identity policies, resource policies, permission boundaries, SCPs, session policies, explicit Denies, and Condition keys may narrow or block this path. The permissions of the target role (passed / assumed / re-trusted) are not in scope and are treated as unknown; this finding does not claim what that role can do, only that this policy would let the principal reach it.",
          "remediation": "Scope iam:PassRole to the specific role ARNs that must be passed and pin iam:PassedToService to ec2.amazonaws.com; separate role-passing from ec2 workload-creation duties, and constrain the passable roles with a permission boundary.",
